<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Riyadh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Riyadh is the capital and largest city of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> region of central Arabia. A small oasis town, it was taken by the First Saudi State in 1773 and became the capital of the second Saudi state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turki-ibn-abdullah">Turki ibn Abdullah</a> in 1824. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Abd al-Aziz ibn Saud</a>'s recapture of it from the Rashidis in 1902 began the making of modern Saudi Arabia, and oil wealth turned it into a city of millions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/riyadh.jpg" alt="Riyadh" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B.alotaby · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riyadh_Skyline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Riyadh is the largest city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>. It lies in the eastern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> plateau, at an average elevation of 600 metres above sea level. The climate is hot desert, with long, extremely hot summers and short, cold winters. The city is known for dust storms so thick that visibility can fall under 10 m. The old town within the walls did not exceed an area of 1 km2, and the city is now divided into fourteen branch municipalities and the Diplomatic Quarter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the pre-Islamic era the city on the site was called Hajr and was reportedly founded by the tribe of Banu Hanifa. It was the capital of the province of Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamama">Yamama</a>, whose governors were responsible for most of central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. Al-Yamama broke away from the Abbasid Caliphate in 866, and the Banu Ukhaidhir, who then ruled the area, moved the capital to nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kharj">Al-Kharj</a>. Hajr went into a long decline. In the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited it, and described it as the main city of Al-Yamama, a city of canals and trees whose inhabitants mostly belonged to the Banu Hanifa.</p><p>Hajr later broke up into separate settlements and estates, the most notable being Migrin and Mi'kal. The earliest known reference to the area as Riyadh, meaning gardens, comes from a 17th-century chronicler describing an event of 1590. Dahham ibn Dawwas, a refugee from neighbouring Manfuhah, took control and built a single wall around the various oasis towns, making them in effect one fortress city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a>, Muhammad bin Saud, then took the town from him, but the First Saudi state they formed collapsed in 1818. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turki-ibn-abdullah">Turki ibn Abdullah</a> founded the Second Saudi state in the early 19th century and made Riyadh his capital, though a joint Ottoman and Rashidi alliance disrupted his rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> retrieved his ancestral rule in 1902 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Riyadh and consolidated it by 1926 with the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>. In September 1932 he named his kingdom Saudi Arabia, with Riyadh as the capital. The first major construction beyond the walls was his Murabba Palace, built in 1936-1938, and the government moved there in 1938. In the 1950s the walls were dismantled and the modern city expanded from the old town. The population was 40,000 in 1935 and 83,000 in 1949. Of the old town the Al-Masmak Palace and parts of the wall and its gate survive, restored.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Riyadh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the fifth century Abikarib As'ad and his son Hassan Yuha'min left an inscription at Ma'sal al-Jumh, west of modern <b>Riyadh</b>, recording an expedition into central Arabia, and the Kingdom of Kinda served as Himyar's client among the Arab tribes of the interior.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In September 2014 the Houthis, tacitly aided by forces loyal to Saleh, seized Sanaa; Hadi fled to Aden and then to <b>Riyadh</b>, and in March 2015 a Saudi-led coalition intervened.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1902 Abdul Rahman's son Abdulaziz, later Ibn Saud, recaptured <b>Riyadh</b> and founded the third Saudi state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The emirate expanded by war and conversion, taking <b>Riyadh</b> in 1773, defeating the Banu Khalid Emirate and conquering al-Hasa.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-diriyah">Emirate of Diriyah</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad ibn Rashid, emir from 1869 to 1897, intervened in the Saudi civil war, took <b>Riyadh</b> in 1887 and won at Mulayda in 1891, becoming master of central Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-jabal-shammar">Emirate of Jabal Shammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Six years after the Ottoman-Egyptian destruction of the Emirate of Diriyah, Turki ibn Abdullah, a grandson of Muhammad ibn Saud, drove the Egyptian garrison from <b>Riyadh</b> in 1824 and made it his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-nejd">Emirate of Nejd</a></li></ul></section>
